:: His round [venue-french char-john] {"position":"300,850","size":"100,100"}
(display: "French Rule SVG h24")</svg>

(set: $sobriety to ($statLoss: $sobriety, 9))\
You nod; he orders two whisky-sodas.

Clocking first, his face, then your own in a pier glass behind him, you observe that you both look much older than you should, had you aged normally or healthily in the time since you stopped speaking.

His eyes shine like slate after cold rain. ‘How’s ASHTON GRANGER?’

You say you wouldn’t know. He’s gracious and decides to press no further.

‘You’re selling,’ he says. ‘I saw you buttering RED.’

JOHN ST. JOHN (if: $savedBy is not "john")[(for that is his name) ]is another poet.<!-- 2026-10-21 (ordering audit): a player beaten in the cellar before this scene has already been rescued by him, and named, there --> He hates RED. His rancour took seed in the days when they both used the same upstairs room in a Bloomsbury pub as their makeshift office.

(link: "⟡ LORE: On Red and John St. John ⟡")[(set: _lore to "On Red and John St. John")(display: "Lore Read")<div class="lore-box"><p>There is a cleaving-apart in English poetics, much as there is in philosophy, which derives from a difference of opinion about the French and the Germans. Where RED isn’t English, he is mostly French. He is Arthur Rimbaud on Charing Cross Road and his poems enact their dérive among the fragments of Mallarmé and Laforgue that Eliot shored up against the ruins in which the Great War left Europe.</p>

<p>In //Socialism: Utopian and Scientific// (1880), Friedrich Engels differentiates between socialists for whom socialism is mostly about acting to make the world better for those who live in it, who are often English, and those whose socialism is rooted in a scientific understanding of ‘will’, as an abstract, impersonal force, subject to the laws of dialectics, not Christian morality.</p>

<p>RED doesn’t know it, but he is of the scientific party. JOHN ST. JOHN, on the other hand, is an English poet, in the way that the Coverdale Bible is an English Bible.</p></div>]
(display: "French Rule SVG h24")</svg>
(link: "Drink with him")[(unless: $drinksRound is a num)[(set: $drinksRound to 0)](set: $sobriety to ($statLoss: $sobriety, 8 + $drinksRound * 5))(set: $confidence to ($statGain: $confidence, 5 + $drinksRound * 2))(set: $drinksRound to $drinksRound + 1)(set: $drinksTonight to $drinksTonight + 1)(if: $sobriety <= 10)[(go-to: "The Blackout")](else:)[(go-to: "The Empty Glass")]]

:: The Empty Glass [venue-french char-john] {"position":"300,950","size":"100,100"}
<span class="empty-glass-marker" style="display:none"></span>
(display: "French Rule SVG h24")</svg>
<script>if(window.showDrinkPopupSafe){window.showDrinkPopupSafe('whisky',400);}else{setTimeout(function(){if(window.showDrinkPopup)window.showDrinkPopup('whisky');},400);}</script>\
After these thoughts you drink and as you drink you notice that JOHN’S glass is drunk.

He drops it; it smashes.

‘Thank God, that was empty,’ he says, and stands.

You reach for your wallet and he waves you off.

‘Next time,’ he says, ‘it comes out in the wash.’

He’s gone and you know that you owe something now, to him and to here.

