if (window.dssAudio) {
  setTimeout(function(){ window.dssAudio.thunderClap(); }, 1800);
  setTimeout(function(){ window.dssAudio.thunderClap(); }, 6800);
}
</script>\
<div class="maritime-scene">\
<div class="maritime-lamp"></div>\
<div class="maritime-lightning"></div>\
<span class="maritime-svg-wrap"><svg viewBox="0 0 1200 18" preserveAspectRatio="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M0,9 C25,2 50,16 75,9 C100,2 125,16 150,9 C175,2 200,16 225,9 C250,2 275,16 300,9 C325,2 350,16 375,9 C400,2 425,16 450,9 C475,2 500,16 525,9 C550,2 575,16 600,9 C625,2 650,16 675,9 C700,2 725,16 750,9 C775,2 800,16 825,9 C850,2 875,16 900,9 C925,2 950,16 975,9 C1000,2 1025,16 1050,9 C1075,2 1100,16 1125,9 C1150,2 1175,16 1200,9" fill="none" stroke="#4a7090" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.8"/></svg></span>

Turning onto Greek Street, you find yourself at sea and in a storm.

<span class="maritime-svg-wrap slow"><svg viewBox="0 0 1200 18" preserveAspectRatio="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M0,12 C30,4 60,16 90,12 C120,4 150,16 180,12 C210,4 240,16 270,12 C300,4 330,16 360,12 C390,4 420,16 450,12 C480,4 510,16 540,12 C570,4 600,16 630,12 C660,4 690,16 720,12 C750,4 780,16 810,12 C840,4 870,16 900,12 C930,4 960,16 990,12 C1020,4 1050,16 1080,12 C1110,4 1140,16 1170,12 C1200,4 1200,16 1200,12" fill="none" stroke="#3a5a78" stroke-width="1.2" opacity="0.9"/></svg></span>

The broiling waters shred your ship till it’s leaf-tobacco, toss it like a stub.

<span class="maritime-svg-wrap"><svg viewBox="0 0 1200 18" preserveAspectRatio="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M0,9 C25,2 50,16 75,9 C100,2 125,16 150,9 C175,2 200,16 225,9 C250,2 275,16 300,9 C325,2 350,16 375,9 C400,2 425,16 450,9 C475,2 500,16 525,9 C550,2 575,16 600,9 C625,2 650,16 675,9 C700,2 725,16 750,9 C775,2 800,16 825,9 C850,2 875,16 900,9 C925,2 950,16 975,9 C1000,2 1025,16 1050,9 C1075,2 1100,16 1125,9 C1150,2 1175,16 1200,9" fill="none" stroke="#4a7090" stroke-width="1.5" opacity="0.8"/></svg></span>

A big hand splits the sky and a big voice booms:

<span class="ne-plus-ultra">NE. PLUS. ULTRA.</span>

Do you keep going?

<!-- RESTORED FORK (2026-07-22): both destinations always on offer — press on to the pub, or answer NE PLUS ULTRA and cross early. Early crossing is safe: Green Sea/LINE 2 is gated behind the page quest at Carthage shore, and The Interval self-guards. The lap is the price. --><!-- NARROWED 2026-09-14 (Sam: "I was able to go straight to Carthage from my first visit to the Pillars, I thought it was meant to be gated"). The gate he asked for went on the in-pub "I can walk on water" link; this interlude was the other way through and had none. The sea-road now opens once he has been inside the pub once, which lines up with the pub link's own gate: on the first trip you go to the Pillars, and both routes open from the second. Delete the (if:) to put the fork back as it was. --><!-- 2026-10-21 (Sam, playtest: "this page should fit on a laptop screen"): the comments here are on one line each and glued to what follows, and the scene's own tags end in \, so the blank lines that stood between them no longer print as empty lines -->\
(unless: $pillarsVisits is a num)[(set: $pillarsVisits to 0)]\
(unless: $inisToldOfPillars is a boolean)[(set: $inisToldOfPillars to false)]\
<!-- PHASES (2026-09-14). The pub itself is only open in two of the night's three Pillars phases: before the critic, and again once page 93 is back with Inis and the third column has been named. In between, the door is shut and the only thing out here is the sea. --><!-- 2026-10-09 (evening), Sam: "I want it gated until you meet Inis and get the little quest for page 93. Otherwise it should take you to the pub." The pub door is open in every phase now; the sea-road waits for O'Flatterly's quest ($knowsAboutPage), not for the critic. --><!-- 2026-10-23 overnight (Sam, playtest: "when you have the key 'seek the shore' should not be emphasised. It should also encourage you to try the third pillar"): with a key in the pocket after INIS's word, Yes (the way to the third pillar) leads and the shore steps back -->(if: $inisToldOfPillars is true and $dreamKey is a string and $dreamKey is not "")[<span class="mar-lead">[[Yes|Approach The Pillars]]<span class="mar-key-chip" aria-hidden="true">⚲ The third pillar</span></span>](else:)[[[Yes|Approach The Pillars]]]
<!-- 2026-10-11 structure pass 3: once Carthage is finished (the page found and the Green Sea's table sat at), the shore only says "You have what you came for" and wakes you, so the sea road closes, as the Carthage shore itself decides. -->(if: $knowsAboutPage is true and not (((history:) contains "LINE 2") and ($hasMissingPage is true or $returnedPage is true)))[//Somewhere out past the breakers, an older shore. //
(if: $inisToldOfPillars is true and $dreamKey is a string and $dreamKey is not "")[<span class="mar-quiet">[[Seek the shore|The coast of Carthage]]</span>](else:)[<!-- 2026-10-29 (Sam, playtest note 9: "When you have received the errand, 'Seek the shore' should be in its own box. Golden, very obviously the choice to make") --><div class="mar-shore">[[Seek the shore|The coast of Carthage]]</div>]]
<!-- A WAY BACK (2026-09-14, Sam: "so you don't have to loop loads"). In the middle phase the pub is shut and the shore was the only thing on offer, so answering NE PLUS ULTRA meant crossing whether you wanted to or not. The storm always lets you turn round now. -->[[Back to Soho|Dean Street]]\
</div>

:: Martin Lackland's Office [char-lack venue-lackland] {"position":"1200,1000","size":"100,100"}
<!-- 2026-10-17 (design note 5.5): a reader cannot read a book that is not in the bag (left, fenced, sold or lent) -->(set: _bagBook to ((($bookAt is not a string) or ($bookAt is "")) and ($bookSold is not true) and (($bookLent is not a string) or ($bookLent is ""))))\
<div id="lk-container"></div>\
<!-- 2026-10-11 structure pass 3: every visit replayed the whole meeting and paid its morale again (+14 or -9), and "Linger" then "Step back" looped it for free morale; the return for the eye replayed it too. The reading and its verdict happen once ($lacklandRead); stepping back from the back door skips the arrival; "Linger" goes once the back room's game is played. -->(set: _lkBack to false)(if: (history:)'s length > 0)[(set: _lkBack to (history:)'s last is "Martin Lackland's Back Door")]\
(unless: _lkBack)[You find the building on Frith Street, on the door: M. LACKLAND.

You knock and he buzzes you in. You climb two doglegs to his rooms.

]<script>setTimeout(function(){ if (window.dssAudio && window.dssAudio.needleLift) window.dssAudio.needleLift(); }, 700);</script>He lifts the needle from his record, sinks his glass then tops it up again.

<!-- 2026-10-17 (Sam's lines; lend the book, design note 5.4): -->(if: $bookLent is "lackland")[(set: $bookLent to "")(set: $bookLentBack to true)Your manuscript’s already on his desk. ‘I was right the first time,’ he says, with the tone of a man breaking news. ‘There’s something here but not enough of it. Come back when you’re finished.’

](link: "⟡ What’s he listening to? ⟡")[<div class="lore-box">
On the turntable was //2 + 2 + 1 = Ponderosa Twins Plus One//, by the Ponderosa Twins Plus One, more specifically the song ‘Bound’, on which the producer Bobby Massey has writing credit.

Massey had been with the O’Jays and would go on, after the turning of the millennium, to be listed as executive producer on a film with the Spice Girls and two films with David Beckham. But he, like the rest of that group, was from Cleveland, Ohio, in the U.S.A.

I’m writing this in February, 2026; if you’re reading it you’ll have lived at least a touch longer. So we’ll know that Ye sample that song in ‘Bound 2’, when he still went by Kanye West. But LACKLAND knows nothing about that.

He has a Garrard 401 screwed into the wall, with an SME arm bolted on and a Shure cartridge. This means that he can play his music loudly.

He found the album at Dobell’s several lunches ago, and it’s hardly been silent since.
</div>]

(unless: ($lacklandRead is true) or (not _bagBook))[He turns to his desk with both his elbows and setting them down individually, says, (if: $metDavy is true)[‘You were sent here by DAVY?’](else-if: $cowRideDone is true)[‘You were sent here by JEFFREY?’](else:)[‘You were sent here, then?’]

You nod.

‘Well, give it here.’

<!-- 2026-10-14 (Sam: pitch the book): you choose to lay it down; "Not yet." keeps it for another visit -->|lkpitch>[(link: "Lay it down")[(set: $lacklandRead to true)<!-- 2026-10-15 (Sam's ruling: the book is fixed; the readers see the man). His near-yes used to want morale 60 and over, which every drink supplies. It now wants the lived night, the Fetch's own gate ($needLilies flowers and $needHaunts haunts, tuned in StoryInit and Start): lay it down early and he sets it down; "Not yet." keeps it for a later visit. -->(unless: $needLilies is a number)[(set: $needLilies to 3)](unless: $needHaunts is a number)[(set: $needHaunts to 6)](set: _lkNear to (($lilyCount is a number) and ($lilyCount >= $needLilies) and ($haunts is an array) and ($haunts's length >= $needHaunts)))(replace: ?lkpitch)[You lay //(print: $bookTitle)// down.

<!-- 2026-10-17 (Sam's line, the worlds wired to the readers): --><span class="dss-gift-line" data-gift="nazca" hidden>He holds the tracing to his lamp, its bulb a gaudy fruit, says ‘Some of my authors are visible only from orbit.’<br><br></span>(if: $nazcaTracing is true)[<span class="dss-gift-now" data-gift="nazca" hidden></span>]He reads the first page; then the last; then a page from somewhere in the middle.

(if: _lkNear)[He sets it down as one might set a toddler down on the floor.

‘This isn’t bad. Have you got a job? A wife? How long can you give to me? You’ve something but there’s work to do; lots of work.’

This is as close as you’ve come to anything.<!-- 2026-10-17 (Sam's lines; lend the book, design note 5.4): -->(if: $bookSold is not true)[

|lklend>[(link: "Leave it with him")[(set: $bookLent to "lackland")(replace: ?lklend)[He files it on a shelf; he knows his system. ‘Come back before they call time tonight. I read everything fast and nothing twice.’]]]]<!-- 2026-10-14 (Sam: a rare sale): on a later night, after SHANA's six months, with enough refusals behind you and the book never lost tonight, you may ask him to take it on (dssBookDeal reveals the link) -->(if: ($bookLostTonight is not true) and ($bookSold is not true) and ($bookDeclined is not true))[<div class="book-deal" hidden>(link: "Ask him to take it on")[(go-to: "The Deal")]</div><script>setTimeout(function(){ if (window.dssBookDeal) window.dssBookDeal(); }, 0);</script>]<span class="dss-refusal" data-k="lackland" data-who="LACKLAND" data-near="1" hidden>‘This isn’t bad. Have you got a job? A wife? How long can you give to me? You’ve something but there’s work to do; lots of work.’</span><span class="dss-refusal-after" data-k="lackland" hidden>Looking up, you count conspicuous Vega, an impossible star through the glossy, lambent smog. Twelve thousand years from now it will have bumped Polaris off the pole; that’s just how the stars fall, how they get lucky. Inside that thought you set yourself to work.</span>

