:: Return the page [venue-cecilcourt char-inis threshold] {"position":"500,1850","size":"100,100"}
You show him 93. O’Flatterly takes it, firmly and gently, then lays it into his papers, where it should be.<!-- 2026-10-17 (Sam's line, the worlds wired to the readers): --><span class="dss-gift-line" data-gift="pyramid" hidden><br><br>‘I hear you, boy! I hear you! I can literally hear you!’ he burrs. ‘Ringing at the pitch of the vibrant scarab!’ Then looking, like a guilty dog, at your book, he adds: ‘But that is not a scarab.’</span>(if: $pyramidNumber is true)[<span class="dss-gift-now" data-gift="pyramid" hidden></span>] He would shelve you, if he could, on his second best bed.

[[‘You don’t know the good that you’ve done’|O'Flatterly's Gift]]

:: O'Flatterly's Gift [venue-cecilcourt char-inis] {"position":"500,1950","size":"100,100"}
<script>/* 2026-10-27 (Sam, note 36): remember the errand is done, so a later night or game can skip Carthage */ try { localStorage.setItem('dssPage93Done', '1'); } catch (e) {}</script><script>setTimeout(function(){ if (window.dssAudio) window.dssAudio.vialClink(); }, 700);</script>\
‘If I may, let me give you small thanks.’

From beneath the counter he produces a small glass vial.

‘This,’ he says, ‘is a slice of the liver of Percy Bysshe Shelley, the poet. It was cut from his body, as it burned on the beach at Viareggio, by his friend Trelawny, who mistook the liver for the heart. It would not burn, you understand. Swallow it when you feel yourself going out, and it will keep you up a while yet.’
